    },
    {
      "condition": "The actor is an intermediary issuing, or substantially involved in issuing, Tokenised Securities which it intends to deal in or advise on, and the supplied intermediary circular applies.",
      "consequence": "That circular expressly retains responsibility for the overall operation of the tokenisation arrangement notwithstanding outsourcing. This is a role-specific allocation rule, not proof of performance, automatic identification of the intermediary as the Product Provider, or a substitute for the requested product circular."
    },
    {
      "condition": "The actor instead deals in, advises on, or manages portfolios investing in Tokenised Securities.",
      "consequence": "The supplied intermediary circular calls for due diligence on issuers and their tokenisation vendors and service providers, and for understanding and satisfaction with their ownership and technology risk controls before related activities begin. These obligations do not by themselves allocate the issuer's overall operational responsibility to that actor."
    },
    {
      "condition": "A supervision event is reliably documented and an applicable standard can be identified for that event.",
      "consequence": "An event-specific assessment can proceed without automatically reconstructing the entire appointment history. Establish the event's date, responsible actor, scope, personnel, information examined, findings, escalation and follow-up. The record may establish what occurred; adequacy depends on its substance measured against the applicable standard. One event does not establish performance throughout an unspecified period."
    },
    {
      "condition": "A reconciliation has been completed and its inputs, population, method and results are reliable.",
      "consequence": "It may establish consistency between the records actually compared. Proper ownership records additionally require legally justified entitlement records, valid rights and transfers, applicable finality and enforceability rules, completeness, treatment of encumbrances, and resolution of discrepancies. Two records can agree while both are incomplete or legally inaccurate. The appendix identifies regular reconciliation between extrinsic-rights records and token records as a consideration, but supplies no universal frequency, tolerance or register hierarchy."
    },
    {
      "condition": "The Handbook's Product Provider requirements apply to the product and actor.",
      "consequence": "Selection of distributors, counterparties and service providers requires the specified care and diligence; key counterparties and service providers require due enquiries into competence and resources to discharge their engagements and comply with applicable product-code requirements. Applicable breach notification, remediation and conflict-management obligations must also be assessed. Appointment alone does not demonstrate these enquiries, and correction does not automatically establish prior compliance."
    },
    {
      "condition": "The intermediary circular's expertise, due-diligence and risk-management expectations apply.",
      "consequence": "Assess manpower and expertise, due skill, care and diligence, and examination of both the underlying product and tokenisation technology. Non-exhaustive appendix considerations include vendor experience, technology audits, justified reliance on third-party smart-contract audits, network robustness and finality risks, interoperability, private-key and cybersecurity controls, ownership protections, enforceability, regulatory approval, continuity, privacy and money-laundering risks. Appropriate technology audits may remain relevant where blockchain is only a secondary record. These considerations are neither an exhaustive checklist nor a safe harbour."
    },
    {
      "condition": "The scheme valuation provisions govern the management company and relevant activity.",
      "consequence": "Assess independent valuation policies developed in consultation with the trustee or custodian, consistent application, pricing-error prevention and correction, periodic management review, and at least annual competent, functionally independent review including testing. Third-party valuation requires care, skill and diligence in selection, appointment and ongoing monitoring, suitable knowledge, experience and resources, and ongoing supervision and periodic review. A single supervisory entry cannot establish these continuing requirements."
    },
    {
      "condition": "Valuation and pricing outcomes are relevant under those scheme provisions.",
      "consequence": "Assess net-asset-value-based offer and redemption prices, fair reflection of asset values, disclosed fees or charges, regular and dealing-day valuation in accordance with constitutive documents, and disclosure of valuation frequency and basis. Necessary fair-value adjustments require due skill, care, diligence and good faith in consultation with the trustee or custodian. Unquoted investments require regular valuation by an approved qualified professional; the management company may perform that role with the requisite approval. Valuation compliance does not itself establish legal ownership-record accuracy."
    },
    {
      "condition": "The supplied management-company acceptability criteria apply.",
      "consequence": "Assess relevant investment experience, demonstrable public-fund track records, sufficient time and attention, and appropriate designated-personnel records. The conditional group-experience route does not remove the individual investment-experience expectation. The general staffing expectation must be read with applicable qualifications, including the incompletely delivered multimanager qualification. Personnel records are not token-holder ownership records."
    },
    {
      "condition": "The securities are in bearer form using permissionless tokens on a public-permissionless network.",
      "consequence": "Heightened cybersecurity, recovery, money-laundering and know-your-client risks require scope-sensitive assessment. Additional non-exhaustive custody considerations concern central-custody immobilisation, transfer time, facility safeguards and compensation capacity, experience, private-key controls, financial resources, conflicts and operational capabilities. Segregation is considered throughout the custody chain against custodian and sub-custodian assets and other customers' assets, subject to the omnibus-client-account qualification. Permitted sub-custodian appointments call for due skill, care and diligence in selection, appointment and monitoring."
    },
    {
      "condition": "The intermediary circular's client-information expectations apply.",
      "consequence": "Assess clear, comprehensible disclosure of material tokenisation information, including settlement finality, transfer restrictions, whether a pre-deployment smart-contract audit occurred, administrative controls, continuity planning and custody where applicable. Disclosure that an audit or control exists does not establish its adequacy or fulfilment of operational responsibility."
    },
    {
      "condition": "Applicable substantive and temporal rules, responsible actors and reliable evidence establish performance across every material assessment interval.",
      "consequence": "A bounded full-period fulfilment conclusion may become justified. If evidence covers only some intervals, limit positive findings accordingly. Missing evidence leaves the affected issue unresolved rather than proving breach. An established defect may defeat fulfilment for the affected duty and interval without establishing failure throughout the entire history. Adequate supervision and correct ownership records must be assessed separately; neither proves the other."
    }
  ],
  "assertion_bindings": [],
  "assumptions": [
    "No assessment dates, provider identities, actual appointments, delegations, migrations, terminations, supervisory events, reconciliation results or operational defects are stipulated.",
    "The delivered source texts are bounded derivatives, not a certified complete or current statement of law. Actor, product, date, authorization conditions and any applicable waivers remain to be established.",
    "The partial readings are proposals, not independent authorities; repeated quotations do not create additional support.",
    "Suggested evidence of authority, supervision and ownership accuracy describes ways to substantiate findings, not a universally prescribed legal checklist. Changes of provider, system or governing rules are possibilities to investigate when relevant, not facts presumed to have occurred."
  ],
  "conclusion": "UNRESOLVED for an unqualified full-period finding. The original requirement correctly identifies missing assessment dates, temporal rules and appointment evidence, but those omissions do not prevent every narrower conditional or event-specific conclusion. The requested product circular's operative responsibility provision and incorporated explanation are not verified in the delivered text. The separate intermediary circular expressly retains overall operational responsibility despite outsourcing for a specified issuance role; this does not establish the Product Provider's precise obligation or its fulfilment. Retained responsibility concerns legal allocation, adequate supervision concerns actual conduct, and proper operational or ownership records concern outcomes measured against an applicable standard. A supervision entry or completed reconciliation alone cannot establish all three. Temporal coverage is necessary for a historical classification but insufficient without the substantive standard, correct actor relationships and reliable factual evidence.",
